In C, a **file** is simply a named sequence of bytes stored on some persistent media (hard drive, SSD, USB stick, network share, etc.).  
C gives you two main ways to work with files:

| Level | What it provides | Typical use |
|-------|------------------|-------------|
| **High‑level (stdio)** | `FILE *` stream objects + functions in `<stdio.h>` (`fopen`, `fclose`, `fprintf`, `fscanf`, `fread`, `fwrite`, `fgets`, `fputs`, `fseek`, `ftell`, …) | Portable, buffered I/O; good for most applications. |
| **Low‑level (POSIX / Win32)** | File descriptors (`int`) + functions in `<unistd.h>` (Linux/macOS) or `<windows.h>` (Windows) (`open`, `read`, `write`, `lseek`, `close`, `fcntl`, …) | Unbuffered, fine‑grained control; useful for performance tuning, non‑blocking I/O, or when you need to mix file I/O with sockets/pipes. |

---

### High‑level (stdio) example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* Open a file for writing ("w") – creates it if it doesn't exist,
       truncates it if it does. */
    FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt", "w");
    if (!fp) {
        perror("fopen");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* Write formatted text */
    fprintf(fp, "Hello, world!\n");

    /* Write raw bytes */
    const char data[] = {0x01, 0x02, 0x03};
    fwrite(data, sizeof(char), sizeof(data), fp);

    /* Close the file */
    if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
        perror("fclose");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

#### Key points

| Function | Purpose | Notes |
|----------|---------|-------|
| `fopen(const char *path, const char *mode)` | Opens a file and returns a `FILE *`. | Mode strings: `"r"` (read), `"w"` (write, truncate), `"a"` (append), `"r+"` (read/write), `"w+"` (read/write, truncate), `"a+"` (read/write, append). Add `"b"` for binary mode (`"rb"`, `"wb"`, …). |
| `fclose(FILE *stream)` | Closes a file. | Flushes buffers; always call it. |
| `fprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...)` | Formatted output. | Similar to `printf`, but writes to a file. |
| `fscanf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...)` | Formatted input. | Reads from a file. |
| `fgets(char *s, int n, FILE *stream)` | Read a line of text. | Stops at newline or EOF. |
| `fputs(const char *s, FILE *stream)` | Write a string. | No formatting. |
| `fread(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream)` | Read raw bytes. | Returns number of items successfully read. |
| `fwrite(const void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream)` | Write raw bytes. | Returns number of items written. |
| `fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int whence)` | Move the file position indicator. | `whence`: `SEEK_SET` (beginning), `SEEK_CUR` (current), `SEEK_END` (end). |
| `ftell(FILE *stream)` | Get current file position. | Useful after `fseek`. |
| `rewind(FILE *stream)` | Reset file position to beginning. | Equivalent to `fseek(stream, 0, SEEK_SET)` plus clearing error flags. |
| `feof(FILE *stream)` | Check for end‑of‑file. | Returns non‑zero if EOF has been reached. |
| `ferror(FILE *stream)` | Check for errors. | Returns non‑zero if an error occurred. |

---

### Low‑level (POSIX) example

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* Open a file for writing (create if missing, truncate if present) */
    int fd = open("example.bin", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    /* Write raw bytes */
    const char data[] = {0x10, 0x20, 0x30};
    ssize_t written = write(fd, data, sizeof(data));
    if (written == -1) {
        perror("write");
        close(fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    /* Seek to the beginning */
    if (lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET) == -1) {
        perror("lseek");
        close(fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    /* Read back the data */
    char buffer[sizeof(data)];
    ssize_t read_bytes = read(fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer));
    if (read_bytes == -1) {
        perror("read");
        close(fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    /* Close the file descriptor */
    if (close(fd) == -1) {
        perror("close");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

#### Key points

| Function | Purpose | Notes |
|----------|---------|-------|
| `open(const char *pathname, int flags, mode_t mode)` | Opens a file and returns a file descriptor (`int`). | Flags: `O_RDONLY`, `O_WRONLY`, `O_RDWR`, `O_CREAT`, `O_EXCL`, `O_APPEND`, `O_TRUNC`, `O_NONBLOCK`, etc. Mode is only used when creating a new file. |
| `close(int fd)` | Closes a file descriptor. | Releases kernel resources. |
| `read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count)` | Reads up to `count` bytes. | Returns number of bytes actually read, or `-1` on error. |
| `write(int fd, const void *buf, size_t count)` | Writes up to `count` bytes. | Returns number of bytes actually written, or `-1` on error. |
| `lseek(int fd, off_t offset, int whence)` | Moves the file offset. | Same `whence` values as `fseek`. |
| `fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ...)` | Manipulates file descriptor flags. | E.g., `F_GETFL`, `F_SETFL`, `F_GETFD`, `F_SETFD`. |
| `stat(const char *pathname, struct stat *buf)` | Retrieves file status information. | Gives size, timestamps, permissions, etc. |
| `unlink(const char *pathname)` | Deletes a file. | Removes directory entry; file contents remain until last descriptor closed. |
| `rename(const char *oldpath, const char *newpath)` | Renames/moves a file. | Works within same filesystem. |

---

### Common pitfalls & best practices

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| Forgetting to close a file | Always call `fclose()` or `close()`; consider `return` statements that guarantee closure. |
| Mixing stdio and low‑level I/O on the same file descriptor | Avoid mixing `FILE *` and `int` on the same underlying file; flush buffers before switching. |
| Not checking return values | Functions like `fopen`, `open`, `read`, `write`, `fclose`, `close` can fail; always test their return codes. |
| Using wrong mode for binary/text | On Windows, use `"rb"`/`"wb"` for binary files; otherwise newline translation may corrupt data. |
| Buffer overflows | When reading into a buffer, ensure you don't exceed its size. |
| End‑of‑file detection | Use `feof()` after a failed read to confirm whether EOF was reached. |
| File permissions | When creating a file, specify appropriate mode bits (`0644`, `0600`, etc.) to enforce security. |

---

### Quick reference table

| Operation | stdio function | POSIX function |
|-----------|----------------|----------------|
| Open for reading | `fopen(path, "r")` | `open(path, O_RDONLY)` |
| Open for writing (truncate) | `fopen(path, "w")` | `open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, mode)` |
| Open for appending | `fopen(path, "a")` | `open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, mode)` |
| Read a line | `fgets(buf, n, stream)` | `read(fd, buf, n)` |
| Write a line | `fputs(str, stream)` | `write(fd, str, strlen(str))` |
| Read raw bytes | `fread(ptr, size, nmemb, stream)` | `read(fd, ptr, count)` |
| Write raw bytes | `fwrite(ptr, size, nmemb, stream)` | `write(fd, ptr, count)` |
| Seek | `fseek(stream, offset, whence)` | `lseek(fd, offset, whence)` |
| Tell | `ftell(stream)` | `lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR)` |
| Close | `fclose(stream)` | `close(fd)` |
| Delete | `remove(path)` | `unlink(path)` |
| Rename | `rename(old, new)` | `rename(old, new)` |

---

### Bottom line

A **file** in C is just a persistent byte stream. You manipulate it through either the high‑level `FILE *` interface (portable, buffered) or the low‑level file descriptor interface (unbuffered, more control). Pick the one that matches your needs, always check return values, and remember to close the file when you're done.
